WEBVTT

00:00.310 --> 00:02.200
- [Instructor] We're told
we want to find the zeros

00:02.200 --> 00:03.810
of this polynomial and they give us

00:03.810 --> 00:06.640
the polynomial right over here,
and it's in factored form.

00:06.640 --> 00:08.840
And they say plot all the zeros,

00:08.840 --> 00:11.300
or the x-intercepts, of the polynomial

00:11.300 --> 00:13.270
in the interactive graph.

00:13.270 --> 00:15.500
And so this is a screenshot
from Khan Academy.

00:15.500 --> 00:16.630
If you're doing it on Khan Academy,

00:16.630 --> 00:19.870
you would click where the
zeros are to plot the zeros,

00:19.870 --> 00:20.950
but I'm just gonna draw it in.

00:20.950 --> 00:23.350
So pause this video and
see if you could have a go

00:23.350 --> 00:26.070
at this before we work on this together.

00:26.070 --> 00:27.990
All right, now let's
work on this together.

00:27.990 --> 00:31.450
So the zeros are the x values

00:31.450 --> 00:35.450
that make our polynomial equal to zero.

00:35.450 --> 00:36.650
So another way to think about it is

00:36.650 --> 00:40.480
for what x values are
p of x equal to zero?

00:40.480 --> 00:42.730
Those would be the zeros.

00:42.730 --> 00:44.080
So essentially, we have to say,

00:44.080 --> 00:47.500
hey, what x values would
make two x times two x

00:47.500 --> 00:50.690
plus three times x minus two,

00:50.690 --> 00:52.620
'cause this is p of x, what x values would

00:52.620 --> 00:54.800
make this equal to zero?

00:54.800 --> 00:56.700
Well, as we've talked
about in previous videos,

00:56.700 --> 01:00.080
if you take the product of
things and that equals zero,

01:00.080 --> 01:02.070
if any one of those things equal zero,

01:02.070 --> 01:03.350
at least one of those things equal zero,

01:03.350 --> 01:05.370
make the whole product equal zero.

01:05.370 --> 01:08.180
So for example, if two x is equal to zero,

01:08.180 --> 01:09.820
it would make the whole thing zero,

01:09.820 --> 01:11.590
so two x could be equal to zero,

01:11.590 --> 01:13.250
and if two x is equal to zero,

01:13.250 --> 01:15.540
that means x is equal to zero,
and you could try that out.

01:15.540 --> 01:18.150
If x is equal to zero, this
part right over here is

01:18.150 --> 01:18.983
going to be equal zero.

01:18.983 --> 01:20.840
Doesn't matter what these
other two things are.

01:20.840 --> 01:22.680
Zero times something times something is

01:22.680 --> 01:24.540
going to be equal to zero.

01:24.540 --> 01:25.490
And then you could say,

01:25.490 --> 01:28.450
well, well maybe two x plus
three is equal to zero,

01:28.450 --> 01:29.410
so we could just write that.

01:29.410 --> 01:31.290
Two x plus three is equal to zero,

01:31.290 --> 01:34.100
and if that were true, what
would x, or what would x

01:34.100 --> 01:35.980
have to be in order to make that true?

01:35.980 --> 01:37.610
Subtract three from both sides,

01:37.610 --> 01:40.000
two x would have to be
equal to negative three,

01:40.000 --> 01:42.710
or x would be equal to negative 3/2.

01:42.710 --> 01:44.050
So this is another x value

01:44.050 --> 01:45.460
that would make the whole thing zero,

01:45.460 --> 01:49.370
'cause if x is equal to negative
3/2, then two x plus three

01:49.370 --> 01:52.440
is equal to zero, you take a
zero times whatever this is

01:52.440 --> 01:54.600
and whatever that is,
you're gonna get zero.

01:54.600 --> 01:56.770
And then last but not least,

01:56.770 --> 01:58.420
x minus two could be equal to zero.

01:58.420 --> 02:01.070
That would make the whole
product equal to zero.

02:01.070 --> 02:04.360
So what x value makes
x minus two equal zero?

02:04.360 --> 02:05.740
We'll add two to both sides,

02:05.740 --> 02:07.700
and you would get x is equal to two.

02:07.700 --> 02:09.910
If x equals two, that equals zero,

02:09.910 --> 02:11.850
doesn't matter what these
other two things are.

02:11.850 --> 02:13.930
Zero times something times something is

02:13.930 --> 02:15.670
going to be equal to zero.

02:15.670 --> 02:19.560
So just like that, we have
the zeros of our polynomial,

02:19.560 --> 02:20.530
and the reason why they have

02:20.530 --> 02:22.880
x-intercepts in parentheses here

02:22.880 --> 02:25.410
is that's where the graph of p of x,

02:25.410 --> 02:27.010
if you say y equals p of x,

02:27.010 --> 02:29.090
that's where it would
intersect the x-axis,

02:29.090 --> 02:30.160
and that's because that's

02:30.160 --> 02:33.120
where our polynomial is equal to zero.

02:33.120 --> 02:35.670
So let's see, we have x equal zero

02:35.670 --> 02:36.940
which is right over there.

02:36.940 --> 02:38.530
Once again, if you're
doing this on Khan Academy,

02:38.530 --> 02:39.680
you would just click right over there

02:39.680 --> 02:41.410
and it would put a little dot there.

02:41.410 --> 02:43.820
We have x is equal to negative 3/2,

02:43.820 --> 02:45.670
which is the same thing as negative 1/2,

02:45.670 --> 02:47.690
so that's right over there.

02:47.690 --> 02:52.690
And then, we have x equals
two, which is right over there.

02:53.000 --> 02:54.330
So those are the x-intercepts

02:54.330 --> 02:57.340
or the zeros of that polynomial.

02:57.340 --> 02:58.930
Now, this is useful in life,

02:58.930 --> 03:02.090
because you could use
it to graph a function.

03:02.090 --> 03:03.880
I don't know exactly what
this function looks like,

03:03.880 --> 03:06.810
maybe it looks something like this,

03:06.810 --> 03:10.470
maybe it looks something like this.

03:10.470 --> 03:12.700
We would have to try out a few
other values to get a sense

03:12.700 --> 03:14.620
of that, but we at least know

03:14.620 --> 03:16.560
where it's intersecting the x-axis.

03:16.560 --> 03:17.913
It's at the zeros.
